        "entity": 17592186166644,
        "text": "∫1(x−1)2x−x2dx=\nSolution:\nFirst, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n2x−x2=−(x2−2x)=−(x2−2⋅x+12−12)=−((x−1)2−1)=1−(x−1)2\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n∫1(x−1)2x−x2dx=∫1(x−1)1−(x−1)2dx.\nLet u=x−1. Then, we have\ndudx=1⟹dx=du.\nTherefore,\n∫1(x−1)2x−x2dx=∫1(x−1)1−(x−1)2dx=∫1u1−u2du=−arsechu+C=−arsech(x−1)+C.\n",
        "hasRawBold": false
      },
      {
        "entity": 17592186166647,
        "text": "∫13−2x−x2dx=\nSolution:\nFirst, we complete the square for the denominator:\n3−2x−x2=−(x2+2x−3)=−(x2+2⋅x+12−12−3)=−((x+1)2−4)=4−(x+1)2\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n∫13−2x−x2dx=∫14−(x+1)2dx=14∫11−(12(x+1))2dx.\nLet u=12(x+1). Then, we have\ndudx=12⟹dx=2du.\nTherefore,\n14∫11−(12(x+1))2dx=14∫11−u2⋅2du=12∫11−u2du=12⋅arcothu+C=12⋅arcoth(12(x+1))+C=12arcoth(x+12)+C.\nNote that this result is subject to the condition |x+12|>1.\n",
        "hasRawBold": false
      }
    ],
